    "body": ">> Conditional statements which occur in the same step are—I think—the only ones which need to be desugared for now:\r\n>>\r\n> I don't think this is correct; I think you'll usually want |foo| to be visible to step 2 of that algorithm, not be scoped to just that item.\r\n\r\nI'm torn. On one side it seems like we really want the equivalent of a ternary operator here (as @jakearchibald mentioned in the OP). On the other it feels very weird (and error prone when refactoring) that the two following statements wouldn't be the same.\r\n\r\n```\r\n1.  If condition, then let |result| be `\"foo\"`.\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\n1.  If condition, then:\r\n    1.  let |result| be `\"foo\"\r\n```",
