    "created_at": "2018-06-14T12:32:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-06-14T17:16:08Z",
    "body": "It's probably easiest to say that, with `[=For each=] |item|`, *item* is scoped to the `<li>` rather than an `<ol>`.\r\n\r\nThat means both of these work:\r\n\r\n```\r\n1. [=For each=] |item| of |list|, do _something_ with |item|.\r\n2. [=For each=] |item| of |list|:\r\n  1. Do _something_ with |item|. \r\n3. This step has no access to |item|.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThat seems pretty close to JS's block scoping.\r\n\r\nThis should be simple-ish for @tabatkins too. When you see `[=For each=]`, the next element sibling should be a `<var>`, which is then scoped to the closest `<li>`.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
